sources:
    - id: gr2000
      title: A Fine Is a Price
      authors: Uri Gneezy and Aldo Rustichini
      year: 2000
      finding: Ten private day-care centres in Haifa, Jan–Jun 1998, 20 weeks. From week 5 a NIS 10 fine per child for coming 10+ minutes late in six centres chosen at random; late pickups rose from about 8 to about 18–20 per centre-week and stayed there after the fine was removed at the start of week 17; the four control centres were unchanged. Weekly averages computed from Table 1.
      url: https://rady.ucsd.edu/_files/faculty-research/uri-gneezy/fine.pdf

    text place "Haifa, 1998\ncloses at 4 pm" #3d3833 x 845 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon clock size 90 x 845 y 430 or ["clock","alarm clock"] icon_look picture of "clock four o'clock" hidden true
    portrait gneezy x 845 y 470 width 190 of "Uri Gneezy" cameo true hidden true
    text eight "≈ 8" #3d3833 at.data [2.5,4] cites ["gr2000"] font_size 48 hidden true
    path m5 dashed #8f887c points [[4.5,0],[4.5,23]] data true hidden true
    text m5t "Fine: 10 shekels\n(≈ $3)" #b5482e cites ["gr2000"] x 845 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text plateau "≈ 18–20" #b5482e at.data [14,23] cites ["gr2000"] font_size 48 hidden true
    path m17 dashed #8f887c points [[16.5,0],[16.5,23]] data true hidden true
    text bet "My bet: fewer" #8f887c x 845 y 330 font_size 30 hidden true
    text m17t "Fine removed" #3d3833 x 845 y 330 font_size 30 hidden true
    text insight "What changed?" #8a5fa8 x 845 y 200 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Do markets change people?"

Can putting a price on something change why people do it? Start in Haifa, Israel, in 1998, at day-care centres that close at four.
    draw axes place

B: And every week some parents turn  (@draw clock@)up late, so a teacher has to stay behind with their child, for no extra pay.

    erase place clock

(@draw gneezy@) The economists Uri Gneezy and Aldo Rustichini counted the late pickups in ten centres fo (@draw line_7 line_8 parallel true@)r twenty weeks. For the first four weeks: about  (@draw eight@)eight a week per centre.

    erase gneezy

B: Then, in six of the ten, picked at random,  (@draw m5 m5t@)a notice went up. Ten shekels, about three dollars, per child, for coming ten minutes late or more.

    erase eight

Textbook economics: make something cost more and people do less of it. I'd  (@draw bet@)bet on fewer late pickups, maybe only a few fewer.

    erase line_7 bet

    ask "Draw what you think happened to late pickups in the fined centres after week 4." on line_1 from 4 store g judge false
        right: Here is what really happened.
        wrong: Here is what really happened.

B: They rose. Within four weeks the fined centres hit about seventeen a we (@draw plateau@)ek, and then settled around eighteen to twenty.

    erase line_8

(@draw line_2@) And the four centres without a fine stayed flat, around nine. So it was the fine, not the weather or a flu going round.

B: Then, at the start of we (@draw m17 m17t@)ek seventeen, the fine was dropped, with no explanation.

So did the parents go back to eight?
    point at.data [18.5,8] gesture circle

    animate stage 1 duration 0.1

B: No. Late pickups stayed near nineteen, more than twice where they started. The fine was gone, the lateness stayed.
    draw line_7 line_8 parallel true

    erase line_1 line_2

So the fine didn't just fail to work. It seems to ha (@draw insight@)ve changed what the parents thought they were doing.

    pause 0.5

## A fine is a price
vars:
    g:
      value: 25
      min: 0
      max: 40
    r:
      value: 25
      min: 0
      max: 40
    f:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 60
sources:
    - id: gr2000
      title: A Fine Is a Price
      authors: Uri Gneezy and Aldo Rustichini
      year: 2000
      finding: 'Two readings of the Haifa result: the fine as information (the worst that can happen is a small fine; an incomplete contract made complete) and as a change of social norm (the teacher''s favour becomes a service bought at a price — ''a fine is a price'', ''once a commodity, always a commodity''). The parking fine in Israel at the time was NIS 75.'
      url: https://rady.ucsd.edu/_files/faculty-research/uri-gneezy/fine.pdf

    text head "What keeps a parent on time?" #3d3833 x 360 y 635 font_size 34 hidden true
    path base #3d3833 points [[180,195],[540,195]] hidden true
    text unit "shekels, illustrative" #8f887c x 360 y 167 font_size 24 hidden true
    path worth dashed #3d3833 points [[180,455],[540,455]] hidden true
    text worth_t "Worth of\nbeing late: 40" #3d3833 x 650 y 455 font_size 30 hidden true
    shape guilt #b5482e shape rect x 360 y 276.25 width 210 height 162.5 bind.height 6.5*(g) bind.y 195+6.5*(0)+6.5*(g)/2 style.fill #e9b8a8 hidden true
    text guilt_t "Guilt 25" #b5482e x 360 y 255 font_size 30 bind.y 195+6.5*(0)+6.5*(g)/2 hidden true
    shape fear #2f6b8f shape rect x 360 y 276.25 width 210 height 162.5 bind.height 6.5*(r) bind.y 195+6.5*(g)+6.5*(r)/2 style.fill #b9d3e2 hidden true
    text fear_t "Fear 25" #2f6b8f x 360 y 255 font_size 30 bind.y 195+6.5*(g)+6.5*(r)/2 hidden true
    shape fine #3d3833 shape rect x 360 y 276.25 width 210 height 162.5 bind.height 6.5*(f) bind.y 195+6.5*(g+r)+6.5*(f)/2 style.fill #cfc8bc hidden true
    text fine_t "Fine {f:0}" #3d3833 x 360 y 255 font_size 30 bind.y 195+6.5*(g+r)+6.5*(f)/2 hidden true
    path ghost50 dashed #8f887c points [[255,195],[255,520],[465,520],[465,195]] hidden true
    text ontime "On time" #2f6b8f x 830 y 250 font_size 44 hidden true
    text late "Late" #b5482e x 830 y 250 font_size 48 hidden true
    text c_price "A fine\nis a price" #b5482e cites ["gr2000"] x 830 y 560 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c_worst "Worst case:\n10 shekels" #2f6b8f cites ["gr2000"] x 830 y 560 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c_comm "“Once a commodity,\nalways a commodity”" #8a5fa8 cites ["gr2000"] x 830 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    text park "Parking ticket\nthen: 75" #8f887c cites ["gr2000"] x 830 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text rule "Norm doing the work\n+ price too small\n= norm crowded out" #8a5fa8 x 830 y 540 font_size 32 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch sc work ["Late if worth > guilt + fear + fine","Before: 40 < 25 + 25 + 0","One brake gone: 40 > 25 + 0 + 10","To deter: 25 + fine > 40"] x 630 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "A fine is a price"

A fine that raised lateness, and kept it raised after it was gone, needs explainin (@draw base@)g. The authors offered two explanations, and both fit.
    draw head

B: Put yourself in a parent's shoes, with illustrative numbe (@draw worth worth_t unit@)rs. Twenty more minutes at work are worth, say, forty shekels to you.

Before the fine, two things hold you back.  (@draw guilt guilt_t@)Guilt: the teacher stays out of kindness, and you shouldn't take advantage of it.

B: And fear. The  (@draw fear fear_t@)contract says nothing about lateness, so who knows what the centre might do if you push it. Together, the (@draw ontime@)y outweigh the forty.

My reading: the fine kills t (@draw c_price fine@)he guilt. Ten shekels turns the teacher's kind (@erase guilt_t@)ness into (@animate g 0 f 10 duration 2@) a service you can buy. Fear alone, twenty-five, plus the fine's (@draw fine_t@) ten is thirty-five (@erase ontime@): u (@draw late@)nder the forty.

    erase c_price

B: Mine is colder. Th (@draw c_worst@)e guilt stays; the fine  (@erase fear_t@)kills  (@animate g 25 r 0 duration 2@)the fear instead. It tells you the worst t (@draw guilt_t@)hat can happen is ten shekels.

    erase c_worst

Either way, one brake goes, and twenty-five plus a ten-shekel price is thirty-five, under a forty-shekel temptation. So you come late.
    highlight worth late

B: (@draw ghost50@) And when the fine is  (@animate f 0 duration 1.5@)dropped, nothing gro (@erase fine_t@)ws back. You can't un-learn that the wors (@draw c_comm@)t is mild, and a favour, once bought, stays a purchase.

    erase c_comm ghost50

Which means a big enough fine should still work. The textbook isn't wrong; it just assumed nothing else changes.
    highlight worth

B: Try it: drag the fine and find where the bar clears the line again.
    explore params ["f"]

In these made-up numbers, any (@draw fine_t@)thing pas (@erase late@)t fi (@draw ontime@)fteen, since one brake is still there; had both gone, it would take more than forty. For scale, a Haif (@draw park@)a parking ticket then cost seventy-five shekels.
    animate f 20 duration 2

    erase park

B: So that's when a price crowds out a (@draw rule@) norm: when the norm was doing the work, and the price is too small to take over.

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: Want the parent's sum in one line?

@math

    erase mathq rule ontime

    fade guilt fear fine guilt_t fine_t worth worth_t base unit head to 0.1

A parent comes late when the worth of the minutes beats everything that holds them back.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

Before the fine: forty is less than twenty-five plus twenty-five. On time.
    draw sc_line_2

With the fine, one brake gone: forty beats twenty-five plus ten. Late.
    draw sc_line_3

So to deter, the fine must top fifteen. If guilt and fear had both gone, it would have to top forty.
    draw sc_line_4

    erase sc

@after

    erase mathq

    fade guilt fear fine guilt_t fine_t worth worth_t base unit head to 1

    draw rule ontime

    pause 0.5

## Do markets change people?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: The harbour council should hire you.
    good: You can tell a price from a message.
    poor: Some prices say more than their number.
    none: No fine for that. Promise.
sources:
    - id: falkszech
      title: Morals and Markets
      authors: Armin Falk and Nora Szech
      year: 2013
      doi: 10.1126/science.1231566
      finding: Subjects could save a surplus lab mouse or take money. Deciding alone, 45.9% accepted the mouse's death for 10 euros; in a bilateral market 72.2% of sellers traded at 10 euros or less (75.9% in a multilateral market). Alone, a similar rate needed 47.50 euros.
    - id: henrich2010
      title: Markets, Religion, Community Size, and the Evolution of Fairness and Punishment
      authors: Joseph Henrich et al.
      year: 2010
      doi: 10.1126/science.1182238
      finding: In experiments in 15 diverse populations, market integration (share of calories purchased) covaries positively with fairer offers to anonymous others.
    - id: montesquieu
      title: The Spirit of the Laws
      authors: Montesquieu
      year: 1748
      finding: Commerce softens and polishes manners ('doux commerce').
    - id: bowles2012
      title: 'Economic Incentives and Social Preferences: Substitutes or Complements?'
      authors: Samuel Bowles and Sandra Polanía-Reyes
      year: 2012
      doi: 10.1257/jel.50.2.368
      finding: 'A survey of about fifty experimental studies: incentives often crowd out social preferences, and sometimes crowd them in.'
    - id: frey1997
      title: 'The Cost of Price Incentives: An Empirical Analysis of Motivation Crowding-Out'
      authors: Bruno S. Frey and Felix Oberholzer-Gee
      year: 1997
      finding: In a Swiss survey, 50.8% accepted a nuclear-waste repository in their community; offered annual compensation, acceptance fell to 24.6%.
    - id: convery2007
      title: The most popular tax in Europe? Lessons from the Irish plastic bags levy
      authors: Frank Convery, Simon McDonnell and Susana Ferreira
      year: 2007
      doi: 10.1007/s10640-006-9059-2
      finding: A 15-cent levy on plastic shopping bags from March 2002 cut their use by roughly 90%.

    text h_left "Markets corrode" #b5482e x 265 y 610 font_size 40 hidden true
    text h_right "Markets civilise" #2f6b8f x 735 y 610 font_size 40 hidden true
    path divider dashed #8f887c points [[500,640],[500,200]] hidden true
    icon mouse size 80 x 265 y 525 or ["rat"] icon_look picture of mouse hidden true
    text alone "Alone: 46%" #3d3833 cites ["falkszech"] x 265 y 445 font_size 44 hidden true
    text market "In a market: 72%" #b5482e cites ["falkszech"] x 265 y 380 font_size 44 hidden true
    text cap "let a mouse die for €10 or less" #8f887c x 265 y 325 font_size 26 hidden true
    text shared "Guilt shared" #b5482e x 265 y 250 font_size 36 hidden true
    text doux "Gentle commerce" #2f6b8f cites ["montesquieu"] x 735 y 530 font_size 40 hidden true
    text mont "Montesquieu, 1700s" #8f887c x 735 y 480 font_size 28 hidden true
    icon globe size 60 x 585 y 395 or ["earth"] icon_look picture of globe hidden true
    text henrich "15 small societies:\nmore market food,\nfairer to strangers" #2f6b8f cites ["henrich2010"] x 775 y 395 font_size 30 hidden true
    text corr "Cause or correlation?" #b5482e x 735 y 265 font_size 34 hidden true
    group board members ["h_left","h_right","divider","mouse","alone","market","cap","shared","doux","mont","globe","henrich","corr"] hidden true
    text s1a "A price can crowd out a norm" #3d3833 cites ["bowles2012"] x 500 y 520 font_size 38 hidden true
    text s1b "mostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 500 y 455 font_size 54 hidden true
    text s2a "Markets make us worse or better" #3d3833 x 500 y 340 font_size 38 hidden true
    text s2b "contested" #b5482e x 500 y 275 font_size 54 hidden true
    group s1 members ["s1a","s1b"] hidden true
    group s2 members ["s2a","s2b"] hidden true

    card title "Do markets change people?"

B: A small fine turned a favour into a purchase. Do markets in general do that to us?
    draw h_left divider h_right

Armin Falk and Nora Szech tested it in Bonn, with surplus lab (@draw mouse@) mice that would otherwise be put down. Each mouse lived only if someone turned down money.

B: Deciding alone, forty (@draw alone@)-six percent took ten euros and let their mouse die. Haggling in  (@draw market@)a market, seventy-two percent sold f (@draw cap@)or ten euros or less.

Their reading: in a market the gu (@draw shared@)ilt is shared. If I don't sell, someone else will.

B: There's an older view, though. In the 1700s  (@draw doux@)Montesquieu argued that commer (@draw mont@)ce makes manners gentler, because you have to deal fairly with strangers.

And there's evidence for it too. Joseph Henrich's team play (@draw globe henrich@)ed sharing games in fifteen small societies, and the more of their food people bought in markets, the more fairly they treated a stranger.

B: That's a correlation, th (@draw corr@)ough. Markets might make people fair, or trade might spread where people already trust each other.

    erase board

So how sure are we? That a price can crowd o (@draw s1@)ut a norm: mostly agreed, from about fifty experiments, which also find prices that strengthen norms.

B: That markets make people worse, or bette (@draw s2@)r, overall: contested. A fine at a day-care centre is not a whole economy.

    quiz "Harbourtown's fishers keep the shared quay tidy out of habit and pride. The harbour council adds a $5 charge for nets left out overnight. What is the risk?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: More nets left out. A small charge can turn a courtesy into a cheap rental, and the pride that kept the quay tidy has nothing left to do.
        wrong: Pride kept the quay tidy, and a small charge turns that courtesy into a cheap rental: here, more nets left out.
        + More nets left out, if $5 reads as a fee for the space
        * No change: $5 is too small to notice
        * Fishers stop using the quay

    quiz "In a Swiss survey, about half of villagers said they would accept a nuclear-waste site nearby. Then each resident was offered a yearly cash payment for it. What happened to support?"
        right: It fell, to about a quarter. Saying yes had been a civic duty; the cash made it look like a bribe.
        wrong: Saying yes was a civic duty, and a payment made it look like a bribe: here, support fell to about a quarter.
        * It rose: money sweetens it
        + It fell, to about a quarter
        * It stayed at about half

    quiz "A town library charges a token 10-cent late fee, and many books come back late. It raises the fee to $5 a week. Going by the parent's bar, what should happen?" voice b
        right: Fewer late returns: a fine too small to do the norm's job backfires, but one big enough to clear the line still deters.
        wrong: Only a fine too small to do the norm's job backfires; one big enough to clear the line still works: here, fewer late returns.
        + Fewer late returns: a big enough price still deters
        * More late returns: fines always backfire
        * No change: the norm is already gone

So do markets change people? Some prices do, because a price also tells you what kind of act you're doing. Send the wrong message, and you keep paying for it, even after the price is gone.
    highlight s1b s2b

    pause 0.5

